# --show-path takes only a selector: not a message, and not a command
! plan --show-path -m "message"
! plan --show-path ls
! plan --show-path search "foo"
! plan ls -m "x"
# Commands take exactly their own arguments
! plan ls extra
! plan search
! plan init a b
-- cmd_1_exit.txt --
2
-- cmd_1_stderr.txt --
plan: --show-path only works with a selector, e.g. `plan --show-path yesterday`.
